Camera Bridge OS @VERSION@: written offer of source code
==========================================================

Camera Bridge OS is a Debian 13 ("trixie") system. Almost everything in it is free software from Debian, and a good part
of that is licensed under the GNU General Public License (GPL), the GNU Lesser General Public License (LGPL) or similar
licenses that give you the right to the source code. This note tells you how to get it.

1. Where the source is

   The exact list of installed packages is in /usr/share/camera-bridge/packages.txt on the running system, and is also
   published with this release on GitHub as "camera-bridge-os-@VERSION@-packages.txt". Each line gives the binary package,
   its version, the Debian source package it was built from and that source package's version.

   If this release is taken off the list of releases later (older beta builds are), its package list and this offer
   are first copied to the release "os-sources" (https://github.com/coalsi/camera-bridge/releases/tag/os-sources) as
   "camera-bridge-os-@VERSION@-packages.txt" and "camera-bridge-os-@VERSION@-SOURCE-OFFER.txt", and they stay there.

   Camera Bridge OS contains these packages unmodified, exactly as Debian publishes them. The corresponding source code
   of every one of them is available from Debian:

     * https://snapshot.debian.org/   (every version Debian ever published, by exact version number)
     * https://deb.debian.org/debian/pool/   (the current archive)
     * https://sources.debian.org/   (browse the source of a package)

   For example, `apt-get source <source package>=<source version>` on a Debian system with deb-src enabled downloads it.

2. If you would rather have it from us

   For at least three years after the day this version of Camera Bridge OS was published, we will give anyone who asks a
   complete machine-readable copy of the corresponding source code of the GPL and LGPL parts, on a physical medium or by
   download. We may charge no more than the cost of doing that. Write to legal@camera-bridge.app and name the version
   ("Camera Bridge OS @VERSION@").

3. Our own programs

   The Camera Bridge programs (camerabridged and the web interface) are published as source at
   https://github.com/coalsi/camera-bridge under the PolyForm Noncommercial License 1.0.0 (see LICENSE next to this
   file). They run ffmpeg as a separate program and do not contain GPL code. Notices for the third-party code they do
   contain are in NOTICE and THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md.

   The streaming helper /usr/lib/camera-bridge/helpers/go2rtc is go2rtc (MIT License, https://github.com/AlexxIT/go2rtc),
   built unmodified from commit c245815e75e2a5fd60b4290f12bfc04e55a984d3 (it reports version 1.9.14+dev.c245815). It is
   not a Debian package and is not in the package list; its source is that commit, and its license text is in
   /usr/share/doc/go2rtc/copyright.

   The helpers /usr/lib/camera-bridge/helpers/wyze-lake (MIT License) and /usr/lib/camera-bridge/helpers/eufy-bridge
   (with the folder helpers/eufy: Node.js, MIT License, and our bundle with the eufy SDK, Apache License 2.0) are our own
   programs; their source is in the same repository under linux/helpers. Their license texts are in
   /usr/share/doc/wyze-lake/ and /usr/lib/camera-bridge/helpers/eufy/LICENSES.

4. Licenses

   Every Debian package keeps its license text in /usr/share/doc/<package>/copyright. A plain-language overview is in
   OS-LICENSES.md next to this file.
